ffmpeg
Lyre ships the ffmpeg program to convert WMA and AIFF files and to measure
loudness. ffmpeg is copyright the FFmpeg developers (https://ffmpeg.org) and
is licensed under the GNU General Public License, version 3 or later
(GPL-3.0-or-later); these builds include GPL libraries such as x264. Lyre
runs it as a separate program and does not change it. It comes from
ffmpeg-static 5.3.0 (https://github.com/eugeneware/ffmpeg-static), release
b6.1.1. The build, its builder and the exact source code, per platform:
  win32-x64: ffmpeg 6.1.1, built by gyan.dev (release essentials build);
    source: https://github.com/FFmpeg/FFmpeg/commit/e38092ef93
  linux-x64: ffmpeg 7.0.2, built by John Van Sickle (johnvansickle.com/ffmpeg);
    source: https://ffmpeg.org/releases/ffmpeg-7.0.2.tar.xz
  linux-arm64: ffmpeg 7.0.2, built by John Van Sickle (johnvansickle.com/ffmpeg);
    source: https://ffmpeg.org/releases/ffmpeg-7.0.2.tar.xz
  darwin-x64: ffmpeg 6.1.1, built by evermeet.cx (Helmut K. C. Tessarek);
    source: https://ffmpeg.org/releases/ffmpeg-6.1.1.tar.xz
The builder's licence text and README (which lists every library in the
build) are installed beside the program, in
resources/app.asar.unpacked/node_modules/ffmpeg-static/ffmpeg.LICENSE and
ffmpeg.README. On request, the project will also send the corresponding
source code of the ffmpeg it ships; ask in the repository above.

Other third-party components keep their own licences. Libraries kept as
packages are listed, with their licence files, under node_modules inside the
installed app's resources folder (app.asar). Libraries built into Lyre's own
code (for example music-metadata, strtok3, React and MUI) are listed with
their licence texts in THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.txt beside each part of the app
(resources/app.asar.unpacked/.webpack/…). This NOTICE and Lyre's LICENSE are
in the installed app's resources folder.
